2 CONT >In recognition of his fathers services, a son, also named Loew Seligmann (born 1735 died 1805), was appointed agent of the Bavarian Court and, on June 28, 1791 received a free pass from the Elector Max Josef of Bavaria. All offic
2 CONC ials, high and low,particularly those of Mainz, the Palatinate, and the free cities of Nuremberg and Augsburg, were re que ted to allow Loew and his two brothers to pass free anywhere, to be exempt from poll tax, and to be given p
2 CONC ermission to carry firearms as a means of defense. The three brothers travelled as money agents or bankers for the Upper Palatine Mint of Arnberg, as their father had done before them. Loew later received a free pass from Sulzoach
2 CONC , where he had two partners. His business affiliations were very extensive. He also received title of Court Purveyer of Fuerth, and there he married Edel, a daughter of Baruch Jafe. Edel died in 1790. Chona Israel, who was a Thora
2 CONC  scholar, and who died in 1816, was one of his sons; another son, Mordechei, died at Munich. Although Seligmann lived in Schnaittach, he seems to have distinguished himself by special serviced in Fuerth. He was very wealthy and in
2 CONC  1764 owned two houses and carried on a very successful grain business. In a letter of safe conduct the Commandant of Kotzingen reports, that Loew Seligmann had given his daughter 1500 gulden to take with her as a marriage gift to
2 CONC  Fuerth. While supplementary taxes were not the custom of the country, it was deemed necessary to ask them in this case because the amount involved was a large. For many years he was the head of the congregation, for which he buil
2 CONC d a school and gave financial support to the Thora scholars.
