1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Married 29/07/1896 in San Rafael, Marin, California. President of the Firemen's Fund Insurance Company from 1917 to 1937.
2 CONT JB's memoir: https://www.biblio.com/book/memories-my-family-j-b-levison/d/1551156084#:~:text=Description,on%20the%20biblio.com%20website.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://oac.cdlib.org/view?docId=hb3c600682&brand=oac4&doc.view=entire_text
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 10 D     Oakland Tribune            Monday, Nov. 24, 1947
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Levison Taken by Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Bertha Levison.  85, financier, civic leader, and patron of music, died in his sleep yesterday at his home at 2420 Pacific Avenue, San Francisco.  Internationally known as an outstanding marine insurance authority, Levison w
2 CONC as regarded for many years as the guiding genius of the Fireman's Fund Insurance Company.  He was one of the founders and a director and president of the San Francisco Musical Association, sustaining organization of the San Franci
2 CONC sco Symphony Orchestra.  He had been a governor of the San Francisco Opera Association and was in charge of music at the 1915 Panama-Pacific International Exposition.   BORN IN NEVADA  Born in Virginia City, Nev., in 1862,  Leviso
2 CONC n came to San Francisco at the age of 13. He entered the insurance business in 1878 and was still active at the time of his death, as a director of Fireman's Fund.  He was a member of many San Francisco clubs and at one time had b
2 CONC een a director of the San Francisco and California Chambers of Commerce.  He had been president of three insurance groups, the Board of Marine Underwriters of the Pacific, Pacific Coast Automobile Underwriters' Conference and Boar
2 CONC d of Fire Underwriters of the Pacific.  MARRIED IN 1896  In July, 1896, Levison married Alice Gerstle, daughter of Lewis Gerstle, a founder of the Alaska Commercial Company, which had pioneered in Alaska following the territory’s 
2 CONC purchase from Russia.  Besides his widow, Levison is survived by four sons, John G., Robert M. and George L. Levison, all of San Francisco, and Charles G. Levison of Pasadena; a sister, Miss Alma Levison of San Francisco; and eigh
2 CONC t grandchildren.  Services will be held tomorrow at 2 p.m., at the Halsted & Company Mortuary, 1123 Sutter Street, San Francisco.  Interment will be private.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
