1 OCCU Actor
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Actor. He was a prolific character actor who appeared in films and television shows, during a career which spanned more than seven decades. He is best remembered by many as 'Homer Bedloe' in the 1960s television co
2 CONC medy series "Petticoat Junction".  Some of his memorable movie roles include, "Mr. Deeds Goes To Town" (1936), "The Cat and the Canary" (1939), "Arsenic and Old Lace" (1944), "It's a Wonderful Life" (1946), "The Juggler" (1953), "
2 CONC Teacher's Pet" (1958), "It's a Mad Mad Mad Mad World" (1963) "The Carpetbaggers" (1964) and "Murphy's Romance" (1985). Beginning in the 1950s, Lane accumulated such television credits as, "I Love Lucy", "Dennis the Menace", "Perry
2 CONC  Mason", "Richard Diamond, Private Detective", "The Beverly Hillbillies", "Get Smart", "The Odd Couple", "Soap", "Hunter" and "L.A. Law". He continued to act into the 1990s. (bio by: [fg.cgi?page=mr&MRid=46798780" target="_blank C
2 CONC .S.)]
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=20387392 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 4 2017, 0:29:17 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Charles Lane (actor) From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Born	Charles Gerstle Levison January 26, 1905 San Francisco, California, U.S. Died	July 9, 2007 (aged 102) Santa Monica, California, U.S. Cause of death Natural Causes Res
2 CONC ting place Home of Peace Cemetery in East Los Angeles, California Occupation	Actor Years active	1931–2007 Religion	Jewish Spouse(s)	Ruth Covell Lane (m. 12/04/1931–2002) (her death) 2 children Charles Lane (born Charles Gerstle Le
2 CONC vison; January 26, 1905 – July 9, 2007[1]) was an American character actor whose career spanned 64 years. Lane turned in his last performance at the age of 90.[2] Lane appeared in many Frank Capra films, including You Can't Take I
2 CONC t With You (1938), Mr. Smith Goes to Washington (1939), Arsenic and Old Lace (1944) and It's a Wonderful Life (1946). He was a favored supporting actor of Lucille Ball, who often used him as a no-nonsense authority figure and come
2 CONC dic foe of her scatterbrained TV character on her TV series I Love Lucy, The Lucy-Desi Comedy Hour and The Lucy Show. His first film of more than 250 movies was as a hotel clerk in Smart Money (1931) starring Edward G. Robinson an
2 CONC d James Cagney. Lane spent a short time as an insurance salesman before taking to the stage at the Pasadena Playhouse. Actor/director Irving Pichel first suggested that Lane go into acting in 1929, and four years later Lane was a 
2 CONC founding member of the Screen Actors Guild. He became a favorite of director Frank Capra, who used him in several films; in It's a Wonderful Life, Lane played a seemingly hard nosed rent collector for the miserly Henry Potter (Lio
2 CONC nel Barrymore), who tried to explain to his employer that many of his tenants were moving out, taking advantage of affordable mortgages provided by the film's protagonist, George Bailey (James Stewart).
2 CONT
2 CONT Lane also appeared in the 1949 film Mighty Joe Young, as one of the reporters cajoling Max O'Hara (Robert Armstrong) for information about the identity of "Mr. Joseph Young", the persona given featured billing on the front of the 
2 CONC building, on opening night.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among his many roles as a character actor, Lane landed the recurring role as newspaper editor Mr. Fosdick in the Peter Lawford sitcom Dear Phoebe, which aired on NBC in the 1954-1955 season. In that same season, Lane played the bo
2 CONC ss of the title character in June Havoc's NBC sitcom entitled Willy.[3] He portrayed Emil Quincy in two episodes of the syndicated romantic comedy series, How to Marry a Millionaire (1957–1959), with Barbara Eden and Merry Anders.
2 CONC  However, he is most widely remembered for his portrayal of J. Homer Bedloe on the television situation comedy Petticoat Junction. Bedloe was a mean-spirited railroad executive who periodically visited the Shady Rest Hotel while s
2 CONC eeking justification to end train service of the Hooterville Cannonball, but he never succeeded in that objective.[2] He guest starred on such series as ABC's Guestward, Ho!, starring Joanne Dru, and The Bing Crosby Show, as well 
2 CONC as the syndicated drama of the American Civil War, Gray Ghost.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a good friend of Lucille Ball, and his specialty in playing scowling, beady-eyed, short tempered, no-nonsense professionals provided the perfect comic foil for Lucy's scatterbrained television character. He played several g
2 CONC uest roles on I Love Lucy, most notably in the episode "Lucy Goes To the Hospital", where he is seated in the waiting room with Ricky while Lucy gives birth to their son.[2] He also played the title role in the episode "The Busine
2 CONC ss Manager", the casting director in "Lucy Tells The Truth. He also played the passport clerk in "Staten Island Ferry." Lane appeared twice in The Lucy-Desi Comedy Hour. He later had recurring roles as shopkeeper Mr. Finch on Denn
2 CONC is the Menace and during the first season (1962–63) of Ball's The Lucy Show, playing banker Mr. Barnsdahl. According to The Lucy Book by Geoffrey Fidelman, Lane was turfed because he had trouble reciting his lines correctly. Howev
2 CONC er, Lane was in reality a placeholder for Lucy's original choice, Gale Gordon, who joined the program in 1963 as Mr. Mooney after he was free from other contractual obligations.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1963, Lane appeared in the mega-comedy It's a Mad, Mad, Mad, Mad World, playing the airport manager. (On the DVD commentary track, historian Michael Schlesinger wryly noted, "You do not have a comedy unless Charles Lane is in i
2 CONC t.") His final acting role was at the age of 101 in 2006's The Night Before Christmas. His last television appearance was at the age of 90, when he appeared in the 1995 Disney TV remake of its 1970 teen comedy The Computer Wore Te
2 CONC nnis Shoes, with Kirk Cameron. In 2005, the TV Land Awards paid tribute to Lane by celebrating his 100th birthday. Seated in a wheelchair in the audience, which had sung Happy Birthday to him, Lane was presented with his award by 
2 CONC Haley Joel Osment and then announced "If you're interested, I'm still available [for work]!" The audience gave him a standing ovation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lane appeared in more than 250 films and hundreds of television shows and was uncredited in many of them. On his busiest days, Lane said he sometimes played more than one role, getting into costume and filming his two or three lin
2 CONC es, then hurrying off to another set for a different costume and a different role.[2] As for being typecast, Lane described it as "... a pain in the ass. You did something that was pretty good, and the picture was pretty good. But
2 CONC  that pedigreed you into that type of part, which I thought was stupid and unfair, too. It didn't give me a chance, but it made the casting easier for the studio." [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lane's persona has been referenced in The Simpsons: on the audio commentary to the episode "Marge in Chains", its director Jim Reardon states that Lane's performance in It's a Wonderful Life inspired the character of the snide, hu
2 CONC mourless Blue-Haired Lawyer who appears in that and other episodes in the series.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life[edit] Lane was born Charles Gerstle Levison in San Francisco, California, to Alice G. and Jacob B. Levison; he was, prior to his death, one of the last remaining survivors of the 1906 San Francisco Earthquake. In 1931, Lane m
2 CONC arried Ruth Covell and they remained together for 70 years until her death in 2002. They had a son named Tom and a daughter named Alice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his stern, hard-hearted demeanor in films and television, friends and acquaintances seem to unanimously describe Lane as a warm, funny and kind person. On January 26, 2007, Lane celebrated his 102nd birthday. He continued 
2 CONC to live in the Brentwood home he bought with Ruth (for $46,000 in 1964) until his death. In the end, his son Tom Lane, said he was talking with his father at 9 p.m. on the evening of Monday, July 9, 2007,[5] "He was lying in bed w
2 CONC ith his eyes real wide open. Then he closed his eyes and stopped breathing." Charles Lane was 102 . He died from natural causes. Lane was not the only person in his family to have a long life - his mother Alice died in her San Fra
2 CONC ncisco home in 1973 aged 100.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also: https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=JW20070720.2.52&e=-------en--20--1--txt-txIN--------
