1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Louise "Wendy" Lilienthal Louise "Wendy" Lilienthal, a vibrant, inspirational, and energetic artist and mother, passed away after a brief illness on November 22, 2015. She was surrounded by her loving family, and d
2 CONC ied a month prior to her 96th birthday. Wendy was born in 1919 in New York. From an early age she was a natural athlete; she remained extremely active throughout her life, playing competitive tennis into her mid-70's. She attended
2 CONC  New York University and the Parsons School of Design before marrying her husband, Theodore (Ted) Max Lilienthal, in 1941. Wendy and Ted moved from New York to San Francisco in 1943, and then to San Anselmo in the mid-1940's. From
2 CONC  the time of her arrival in San Anselmo, Wendy threw herself into community work; she held leadership positions within the Sleepy Hollow Homes Association, Red Cross, Sir Francis Drake High School PTA, Drake Scholarship Foundation
2 CONC , Youth in Arts, and the Marin Society of Artists. At the time of her passing, she was a co-organizer of the Smith Ranch Homes art gallery and a member of the selection committee for the Atrium Gallery at the Marin General Hospita
2 CONC l Cancer Center. In addition to her community work, Wendy distinguished herself as an artist. She worked in a variety of forms, but specialized in handmade papers, recycled materials, imported paper and textiles, and marbling tech
2 CONC niques. She appeared on national PBS television to exhibit her art and demonstrate paper making, and taught in a variety of galleries and colleges. She also founded the group Fibermania, which showcased the work of Bay Area fiber 
2 CONC artists. Her work, which garnered her many awards, was featured in numerous exhibits and publications, and was commissioned by several hospitals, including Kaiser Permanente. It was also exhibited nationally and internationally in
2 CONC  private and corporate collections, galleries, and museums, including the M.H. De Young Museum. Throughout their life together, Wendy and Ted shared a passion for travel and the arts. They held season tickets at the San Francisco 
2 CONC Symphony and Opera and traveled extensively, visiting art and music venues throughout the world. They also treasured their family. For over 60 years, Wendy and Ted hosted gatherings at their home, first in Sleepy Hollow and then a
2 CONC t Smith Ranch, for both small and large occasions. Wendy was preceded in death by Ted and her sister Betty. She is survived by three children: Peggy Linden, Tom Lilienthal (Patricia), and Vicky Rivera Lilienthal (Felix Rivera - de
2 CONC ceased), five grandchildren, and six great-grandchildren. The family is grateful for the love and exceptional care provided by Wendy's caregivers, Meli and Vaciseva Ganilau. In lieu of flowers, donation can be made to The Ritter C
2 CONC enter at www.rittercenter.org.
2 CONT
2 CONT Published in Marin Independent Journal on Dec. 12, 2015
2 CONT
2 CONT - See more at: http://www.legacy.com/obituaries/marinij/obituary.aspx?n=louise-lilienthal-wendy&pid=176864401#sthash.PSurCs7D.dpuf
2 CONT
2 CONT - See also http://www.fiberdimensions.com/lilienthal/index.html 
