2 NOTE W.G.Bates connections with the academic and business worlds brings up the age-old question: Is one's ideology a product of  one's economic interests.
3 CONT I propose this having traced the scholarship in Bates' Columbia back in Manhattan. Yes, Columbia was on Madison Ave. in those days. Bates' cohorts, those who spoke at his graduation and those who taught and wrote later included so
3 CONC me fascinating theorists. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_A._Beard is one. Beard's https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/An_Economic_Interpretation_of_the_Constitution_of_the_United_States bears on this topic of Walnut Grove in that
3 CONC  the attitudes about Man v. Nature combined with Man as an ambitious miner of the "new" West comes to the fore.
3 CONT
3 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/An_Economic_Interpretation_of_the_Constitution_of_the_United_States would be an interesting book to read
2 NOTE Returning to the topic of the naming of Lake Seligman:
3 CONT Walter G. Bates undoubtably knew of fellow alum. Seligman spent his entire academic career at Columbia University, first joining as a lecturer in 1885."Edwin Robert Anderson Seligman (1861–1939), was an American economist who spen
3 CONC t his entire academic career at Columbia University in New York City. "Seligman is best remembered for his pioneering work involving taxation and public finance."
3 CONT As such, Seligman would have lectured Bates as an undergrad.
3 CONT It should be remember that Edwin Seligman was a son of the titan of the Seligman family, https://www.geni.com/people/Joseph-Seligman/6000000010534100463 
