1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was born Isaak Seligmann in Baiersdorf, Erlangen-Hochstadt, Bayern, Germany (Bavaria), to David Isaak Seligman and Fanny Steinhardt. He later changed his name to Isaac, and in August 1857, at the age of 23, Seli
2 CONC gman joined his entrepreneurial brothers in the United States. Seligman went on to run 'Seligman Brothers', the London branch of the Seligman merchant-banking empire with his brother Leopold.[1] He married 18-year-old Lina Messel 
2 CONC (b. Darmstadt 1851) in London in 1869. Between 1869 and 1886, Lena bore him three daughters and four sons, the eldest son being Charles David Seligman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Seligman was also a fundraiser for, benefactor to, and activist in, a large number of charitable and political organisations including the American Society in London, the Anglo-Jewish Association (lobbying against oppression
2 CONC  of Serbian Jews), the German Association (raising funds for those wounded or made destitute by the Franco-Prussian War), the Mansion House Committee (raising funds for distressed Jews in Russia), the Eighty Club in London (social
2 CONC  and political), and the Jew's Deaf and Dumb Home (lip-reading for deaf and dumb), originally founded by Baroness M. de Rothschild, of which home Seligman was the treasurer in 1875.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1896, Seligman was appointed joint legal owner and trustee of the 'Tregullow Offices' (later Zimapan Villa), a former Cornish mine office belonging to the Williams mining-mogul family of Scorrier, Cornwall, by Charles Augustus 
2 CONC Vansittart Conybeare, barrister-at-law and MP for Camborne, Cornwall (1885-1895).[3] Seligman was released from his trusteeship in 1902 when Charles Conybeare and his wife Florence sold the property, which originally formed part o
2 CONC f a marriage settlement, to mining engineer Charles Rule Williams,
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1899, Seligman bought 17 Kensington Palace Gardens, London,[2] [5] a grand mansion built in the north Italian villa style, near Arthur Strauss MP (Charles Conybeare's parliamentary successor), who lived down at the end of the t
2 CONC ree-lined boulevard at No. 1 Kensington Palace Gardens. At that time, Seligman's principal home, now part of London's billionaire's row, had at least four reception rooms and 13 bedrooms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seligman died a wealthy man in 1928 at the age of 93, leaving a fortune in his will valued at more than ₤18 million in year 2000 money.
