3 STAE New York
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 JUN 1954
2 ADDR
3 CITY Shenandoah
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Seligman Lewisohn, 1895 – 1954
2 CONT
2 CONT by Barbara L. Tischler
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Seligman Lewisohn—education advocate, philanthropist, art collector, and college trustee—was born in New York City on February 14, 1895. She was the daughter of Isaac Newton and Greta (Loeb) Seligman. Lewisohn came from a
2 CONC  prominent German Jewish family. Her father headed J. & W. Seligman, the bank that served as the fiscal agent for the Union during the Civil War, and her mother’s father founded the firm of Kuhn, Loeb and Company.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Lewisohn graduated from Miss Masters School in Dobbs Ferry, New York, in 1912. An aspiring pianist, she attended the Institute of Musical Art (later known as the Juilliard School of Music), earning a degree in 1914. Durin
2 CONC g her two years of conservatory study, Lewisohn also pursued course work at Teachers College of Columbia University, where she was particularly interested in the progressive education ideas of John Dewey and others. Her interest i
2 CONC n education was, in part, an outgrowth of her family’s long-standing connection to the Ethical Culture School. At the same time, Lewisohn worked at the Hudson Guild Settlement House, where she also performed as a pianist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Lewisohn was married to Sam A. Lewisohn, son of Adolph Lewisohn, benefactor of City College and other major New York cultural institutions, on February 2, 1918. The couple collected major works of art, including a substan
2 CONC tial number from the modern period. They resided on East End Avenue in Manhattan and in Harrison, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT Calling herself “a citizen interested in education,” Lewisohn had a long and illustrious career acting in the interests of public education, including membership in the Public Education Association, which she joined in 1922. In 19
2 CONC 41, she became the professional director of the organization, which worked with the city’s board of education to improve the quality of teachers, school facilities, and curricula. From 1946 to her death, she served as the chair of
2 CONC  the association’s board of trustees. She often wrote of her views on education, arguing that “the schools will only be as good as we citizens desire them to be.... We must continue to create an aroused public opinion that will de
2 CONC mand the best in education and that will be willing to pay for it now.”
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to her professional work in education, Lewisohn devoted her time and energy to a number of New York City’s most prominent educational and cultural institutions. From 1930 to 1936, she served as the chair of the Women’s
2 CONC  City Club education committee, which devoted its energies to studying the progress of so-called super-normal and sub-normal children in the New York City public schools. She served as chair of the board of trustees of the Little 
2 CONC Red School House from 1936 to 1940 and was a member of the Museum of Modern Art’s education committee from 1936 to 1944. From 1939 to 1946, Lewisohn was a trustee of Bennington College, which two of her four daughters attended, an
2 CONC d she was a trustee of Vassar College at the time of her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewisohn was killed in an automobile accident on New York’s Taconic State Parkway near the small town of Shenandoah on June 14, 1954. She was on her way home from a visit with Adlai Stevenson, the 1952 Democratic presidential cand
2 CONC idate, who had presented the commencement address at Vassar. Lewisohn was fifty-nine years old and was survived by four daughters: Marjorie Lewisohn, Mrs. Sidney Simon, Mrs. Julian Eisenstein, and Mrs. Ernest Kahn. Her memorial se
2 CONC rvice at Temple Emanu-El in New York City drew more than five hundred prominent citizens. In her will, she bequeathed major works to the Metropolitan Museum of Art, the Museum of Modern Art, the National Gallery, the Brooklyn Inst
2 CONC itute of Arts and Sciences, Vassar College, and the Whitney Museum of American Art.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT AJYB 56:570; EJ; Obituary. NYTimes, June 15, 1954, 1:7; WWIAJ (1938); WWWIA 3.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
