1 NAME William Wolf /Haas/
2 GIVN William Wolf
2 _MARNM Haas
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Home of Peace Cemetery, Section 4, Plot J, Lot 1
2 ADDR
3 CITY Colma
3 STAE CA
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 APR 1849
2 ADDR
3 CITY Reckendorf
3 STAE Bayern
3 CTRY Deutschland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 31 MAY 1916
2 ADDR
3 CITY San Francisco
3 STAE CA
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William Haas was born in the village of Reckendorf, Bavaria on April 24, 1849 to a large family of modest means. Little is known about Haas’ parents, other than his father’s career as a weaver. William Haas (christ
2 CONC ened [sic] Wolf) was the youngest of nine children.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1865, a 16-year-old William and older brother Abraham sailed to New York City. Abraham started a business venture in Los Angeles and William lived briefly in Missouri, and then tried mining in the Boise Basin and Silver City ar
2 CONC eas of Idaho. William arrived in San Francisco on October 9, 1868. He joined the wholesale merchant grocery firm of Loupe & Haas where his cousin Kalman was one of the partners. In 1869, William’s address was recorded as the Nucle
2 CONC us Hotel at Third and Market streets. After Leopold Loupe, partner of Loupe & Haas, retired a rich man in 1875, the firm became Haas Bros. with offices at 100-2 California Street at Davis Street. The firm prospered and in 1897 it 
2 CONC became incorporated with William as its first president.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1880, William married Bertha Greenebaum, the 19-year-old daughter of Herman and Rosalie Greenebaum, a prominent German-Jewish family in San Francisco. At the time of Bertha’s marriage to William Haas, the Greenebaum family was 
2 CONC living at 1917 Franklin Street, just down the street from 2007 Franklin Street. For the first few years of their marriage, William and Bertha lived in a succession of residences including a house on Van Ness near Pacific Street be
2 CONC fore building their family home at 2007 Franklin Street in 1886. William and Bertha had three children: Florine (October 7, 1881), Charles William (February 16, 1884), and Alice (March 21, 1885).
2 CONT
2 CONT Both William and Bertha led active social lives, particularly within the Jewish community. They were members of Temple Emanu-El and were busy with many charitable and cultural activities. During his lifetime, William held posts as
2 CONC  a director of Wells Fargo, as president of Mt. Zion hospital, and on the Board of arbitration of the San Francisco Chamber of Commerce. He was one of the largest contributors to the Federation of Jewish Charities. Bertha Haas ser
2 CONC ved as one of the first directors of the Emanu-El Sisterhood of Personal Service (est. 1894), which founded the successful Boarding Home for Jewish Working Girls. She was a member of the Council of Jewish Women and, like her husba
2 CONC nd, served as a director of Mt. Zion hospital. She also worked zealously to establish the Philomath Club Jewish women’s organization.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Haas died very suddenly on May 31, 1916 after a full day of work. A local Rabbi wrote that his death “created a void in the ranks of merchant princes and philanthropists in San Francisco.” William left a varied portfolio o
2 CONC f stocks in banks, sugar companies, insurance, mines, utilities, and manufacturing companies. His son, Charles William Haas became the new president of Haas Bros. From http://www.sfheritage.org/haas-lilienthal-house/haas-lilientha
2 CONC l-house-history/
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT See also:
2 CONT https://www.haas-lilienthalhouse.org/_files/ugd/abba95_926bd9fa8b54458f9c600aada5437604.pdf
