1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The parents of Abraham Gerstle can not be verified as source documents are not available. They might be Solomon Isak Gerstle and his wife Hendel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between the "ancient" Gerstles, there are possible four Abrahams:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.	Abraham Gerstle, father of Isak Michael Gerstle and Hayum Gerstle. His wife was named Frumet. According to the book: "Our City: The Jews of San Francisco" he lived between 1740 to 1796. A private tree in ancestry.com (which doe
2 CONC s not reveal any source) tells that his father's name was Akiva Gerstein.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.	Abraham Gerstle, the brother of Gerson Gerstle, died in November 1791 (according to Meyer Ellinger's tree).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.	Abraham Gerstle, c.1775-1849. His wife was named Rösle. The fact that the names Gerson and Isak do not appear in his family tree, makes the connection difficult. Maybe he is descended from Reuben in some other way.  Another opt
2 CONC ion is that he took the name Gerstle at a later time, even though he was unrelated. Only a Y DNA test of descendants of different branches could reveal the truth.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.	Abraham Gerstle, father of Leopold (Löb) Gerstle. Leopold lived between c.1759 to 1833. Leopold had a son named Abraham born on 2nd January 1792. This causes me to suspect that the Abraham who died in November 1791 was his fath
2 CONC er. Leopold must be descendant of Gerson Samuel, as he had a son named Gerson who was born in 1797.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is a bit strange that the Abraham whose wife was called Frumet had children when he was 47 and 49. There are two possible solutions:
2 CONT
2 CONT His birth date is incorrectly shown (too early).
2 CONT
2 CONT Frumet was his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Hayum repeats both (and only) in Isak Michael's and Leopold's branches. This fact might give us a clue that they were brothers, and maybe Frumet's father was named Hayum.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maybe there is a mistake and there is only one ancient Abraham, but it is still not 100% certain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Analysis by Omer Strassmann. June 2019
