1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Managers of Adolph Samson Bissinger,  08/20/2018
2 CONT
2 CONT I am contacting you about this profile: https://www.geni.com/people/Adolph-Bissinger/6000000008199751403
2 CONT
2 CONT Greetings, I have relatively minor corrections regarding the location of the train accident (it wasn't a collision, it was a landslide across the tracks). The location of death for both Adolph and his wife Minnie was not Wells, El
2 CONC ko County, NV. They died near at Mill Creek (now a ghost town) in Pershing County, NV. It is near present day Winnemucca, NV. Mill Creek is reported in all of the news articles as the location and Wells, NV is not mentioned. Wells
2 CONC  has been erroneously stated in several on line sources.  Thanks very much for your work on this.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sincerely,
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Denise
2 CONT
2 CONT DISASTER TO THE CENTRAL PACIFIC'S EAST-BOUND FLYER COSTS SIX LIVES.
2 CONT
2 CONT Winnemucca, Nev., Feb. 17. -- Six persons perished and six others were seriously injured in a disaster that occurred to Central Pacific east-bound flyer No. 2 two miles east of Mill City at an early hour this morning. While runnin
2 CONC g at high speed to make up lost time the flyer crashed into a washout that had been caused by a mountain torrent. The shock came without the slightest warning, and the engine and train crews and sleeping car inmates at the forward
2 CONC  end of the train were pinned beneath the debris of the locomotive tender and cars that were twisted into a tangled mass of wreckage in an instant by their own impetus. Of the six persons who perished, it is probable that no one h
2 CONC ad time to realize the afte that was upon them. Three of them -- MR. and MRS. ADOLPH BISSINGER and CLINTON R. COULTER -- were San Franciscans occupying berths in the forward Pullman. Of the other victims of the disaster, one was F
2 CONC ireman WHITAKER, who died at his post, and the others were tramps stealing a ride on the train. At the point where the accident occurred, there is an embankment of thirty feet to a ravine down which water runs from a water shed of
2 CONC  fifteen miles. During the past two weeks heavy and frequent snowstorms have occurred in the mountains, piling the snow into immense drifts in the canyons. This began to melt because of the unusually warm weather of Friday and Sat
2 CONC urday. Gangs of workmen have been laboring since yesterday morning endeavoring to keep the roadbed in the vicinity of Mill City in good condition. At 11 o'clock last night a washout was discovered a half mile west of Mill City and
2 CONC  all energy was put toward reparing it. This was not accomplished until 4:30 o'clock this morning. Passenger trains Nos. 2 and 4 had been held at Humboldt, nine miles distant, while the break as being repaired. Because of being la
2 CONC te the flyer was making a fast run, endeavoring to make up time. It was going at the rate of fifty miles an hour at the time of the accident. When two miles east of Mill City it struck a new washout that evidently was caused by a 
2 CONC mountain torrent only a short time before the train's arrival. The engine got across safely, but the tender is one mass of twisted iron, standing on and against the boilerhead of the locomotive. The trucks of the tender, together 
2 CONC with those of the mail car and composite car, are in the torrent below. The mail car went to the left, the rear end being clear of the head end of the engine. The composite car lies across the gap of seventy-five feet and telescop
2 CONC es the head Pullman car nearly thirty feet. Engineer ABBAY says that he felt the engine settling and started to get out through the window, but he had hardly moved from his seat before the crash came. How he got out he doesn't kno
2 CONC w. Conductor MARKLE, Brakeman REEVES and the train barber were in the composite car. Brakeman REEVES jumped from a window, landing in the creek below, which was at that time about four feet deep. MR. BISSINGER of the firm of Bissi
2 CONC nger & Schloss, hide and tallow merchants of San Francisco, occupied a drawing room with his wife and daughter, ELSIE, at the head end of the first sleeper. This car was telescoped by the buffet. Both MR. and MRS. BISSINGER were k
2 CONC illed, while their daughter, occupying the berth above them, was but slightly injured. Mill City, NV Train Into Washout, Feb 1901
2 CONT
2 CONT Submitted by Stu Beitler on 7 August 2010 - 6:54pm Nevada Train Wrecks and Accidents 1901 Birth, Marriage & Death Records
2 CONT
2 CONT MR. BISSINGER and family were en route to New York. As soon as MISS BISSINGER recovered from the shock she was astir, and searching parties found her, together with Conductor MARKLE, and helped them out through a small opening in 
2 CONC the roof of the car. CLINTON R. COULTER had evidently been killed outright. When found he was doubled up, with a large hole in one side of the head, back of the car. The deceased had a ticket to Chicago and return. Fireman WHITAKE
2 CONC R met death at his post of duty, probably never knowing what had happened. His body is pinned under the debris of iron and can be plainly seen; yet it is in such a mass of wreckage that up to 8 o'clock tonight he could not be got 
2 CONC out. The body was terribly scalded. Two bodies, evidently those of tramps, are in the mass of twisted iron. One lies near that of the fireman, the left hand protruding far enough to be visible, while the other is in the creek, pin
2 CONC ioned under one of the car trucks. How Mail Clerks Scuyler and Cavin escaped with their lives is an enigma to those who saw the position of the wreckage, yet their injuries are very slight. No. 4, the eastbound express, was follow
2 CONC ing the overland limited closely. The rear brakeman on No. 2 had but a few minutes time to flag it to prevent a rear-end collision. Telegrams were sent to an uncle of MISS BISSINGER at the Hotel Richelieu, in San Francisco, notify
2 CONC ing him of the deaths of his relatives and requesting him to meet his niece. Mrs. Coulter, the widow of CLINTON R. COULTER, was telegraphed to at Millwood, Cal., informing her of the sad death of her husband. The Dead: ADOLPH BISS
2 CONC INGER, aged 40 years, of Bissinger & Schloss, San Francisco. MRS. ADOLPH BISSINGER, aged 30 years, of San Francisco. C. L. WHITAKER, fireman, aged 25 years, of Wadsworth, Nev. CLINTON R. COULTER, aged about 35 years, of San Franci
2 CONC sco. Two tramps, names unkonwn. The Injured: MISS ELSIE BISSINGER, 16 years of age, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Adolph Bissinger, slightly bruised. Conductor H. E. MARKLE, of Ogden, scalp wounds and face and body bruises. Brakeman J.
2 CONC  J. REEVES, of Ogden, slight body bruises. Engineer GEORGE ABBAY, of Wadsworth, head and face scalded, shoulder wrenched and left foot injured. Mail Clerk SCHUYLER, of San Francisco, right leg broken and cut about the head. Mail C
2 CONC lerk CAVIN, of San Francisco, bruised about the body and cut on the head.
2 CONT
2 CONT The San Franciscan Sun California 1901-02-18
