1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.ctinsider.com/news/article/DINKELSPIEL-Clara-Bransten-2680442.php:
2 CONT DINKELSPIEL, Clara Bransten - Peacefully in Portola Valley, October 16, 2004, age 94. Predeceased by her beloved husband, of 67 years, John W. Dinkelspiel; loving mother of Jean D. Chaitin and mother-in-law of Neil D. Chaitin; dev
2 CONC oted grandmother of Kenneth, Katherine and Anne Chaitin. A fourth-generation San Franciscan, she and John shared a passion for fly fishing. She had a deep interest in American history and the environment and was a long-time volunt
2 CONC eer at the Atherton Public Library, the Stanford University Art Gallery and Filoli. At her request, no Services were held. Memorial contributions may be made to Pathways Hospice Foundation, 201 San Antonio Circle, Ste. 104, Mounta
2 CONC in View CA 94040.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.almanacnews.com/news/2008/04/03/clara-dinkelspiel-lived-in-atherton-almost-80-years/
2 CONT
2 CONT Clara Dinkelspiel lived in Atherton almost 80 years
2 CONT
2 CONT Clara Bransten Dinkelspiel, who lived in Atherton almost 80 years and was the wife of the late town judge and councilman, John Dinkelspiel, died peacefully in Portola Valley October 16. She was 94.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Dinkelspiel was widely known as a lively, energetic woman who threw herself into causes such as the Atherton Library, Filoli and the Stanford Art Gallery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having moved to Atherton in 1913 at age 3, she was always bubbling with stories of halcyon summers riding ponies, doing drills for events at the Circus Club, and swimming at the James Flood pool in Lindenwood.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mrs. D. was my second mother," said Judy Harris of San Mateo, grand-daughter of Clarence Walter, who drilled local kids to perform in community circuses that evolved into today's Menlo Circus Club and Tally Ho benefit for Childre
2 CONC n's Hospital at Stanford. "She was a real history buff, very well-read, and told wonderful stories."
2 CONT
2 CONT Clara Bransten, a fourth-generation San Franciscan, was born in 1909 into the family that founded MJB Coffee. When her father had a brush with tuberculosis, the family moved down the Peninsula, where the climate was better. Unlike
2 CONC  their friends who came down in the summers to escape the fog, Clara and her family lived in Atherton year-round.
2 CONT
2 CONT In an oral history recorded by Atherton historian Sally Bush, Mrs. Dinkespiel recalled getting her first pony and cart for her 6th birthday. "My pony was a mode of transportation for me," she said.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Dinkespiel described busy summers spent riding -- always bareback -- sliding down a haystack, and swimming with her San Francisco buddies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Especially exciting was practicing for the kids' circus. Ringmaster Clarence Walter kept the children drilling stunts and quadrilles by promising ice cream. "He was a taskmaster. If you did it right, you got an ice cream cone," sa
2 CONC id his granddaughter, Mrs. Harris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Winter was more routine. Clara first attended Miss Harker's Seminary for Girls, and, later, Castilleja. She said that starting when she was 8, she would sometimes drive herself to school in an automobile. "Father taught me to driv
2 CONC e on his lap when I was 4," she recalled.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1931, Clara Bransten married John Dinkespiel, a promising young attorney who went on to be town judge in Atherton, and later a much beloved councilman and mayor. "John handled the only murder trial in Atherton," recalled former
2 CONC  councilwoman Nan Chapman.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Atherton, Mrs. Dinkelspiel became very involved in the community. "She had a lot of energy and curiosity," commented longtime Atherton friend, Elizabeth Gerstley.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Clara Dinkelspiel shared their passion for fly fishing with Jim and Elizabeth Gerstley on numerous trips to the Makenzie River in Oregon and elsewhere. "We shared many fish fries together," said Mrs. Gerstley.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1992, John and Clara Dinkelspiel moved to The Sequoias in Portola Valley. Mr. Dinkelspiel died in 1998.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Dinkelspiel is survived by a daughter, Jean D. Chaitin of San Francisco, and three grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT At her request, no services were held. The Neptune Society handled arrangements.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family suggests memorial contributions to Pathways Hospice Foundation, 201 San Antonio Circle, Suite 101, Mountain View, CA 94040.
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.almanacnews.com/morgue/2004/2004_11_03.obit.shtml
2 CONT
2 CONT See also: https://www.almanacnews.com/news/2008/04/03/clara-dinkelspiel-lived-in-atherton-almost-80-years-
