1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Michael Wornum Democratic Date	Party	Office	Votes	Result 11-05-1974	Democratic	AD-09	47758	Win 11-02-1976	Democratic	AD-09	59079	Win 11-07-1978	Democratic	AD-09	46485	Loss
2 CONT
2 CONT Candidate Biography:
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: February 25, 1925 in London, England Married: Barbara Okin, Sandra Children: Amanda, Christopher, Claudia, and George Military Service: Royal Air Force Died: June 1995
2 CONT
2 CONT Previous: Member, Mill Valley City Council Previous: Mayor, City of Mill Valley Previous-1974: Member, Marin County Board of Supervisors
2 CONT
2 CONT Several of his paintings have appeared on Ebay. PLACENAME: Wornum Drive in Corte Madera was named for Michael. Source: California Blue Book (1975)
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Wornum, a gregarious, conservation-minded Marin County politician for more than 30 years, died of stomach and liver cancer at his home in Larkspur yesterday. He was 70 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT SF GATE Friday, June 16, 1995
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wornum, a Democrat, was mayor of both Mill Valley and Larkspur. For six years, he was a Marin County supervisor. He was also chairman of the California Coastal Commission.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wornum was elected to the state assembly in 1974. He was re- elected in 1976, but two years later was defeated by Republican William Filante.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Early on, he was an advocate of some great liberal ideas -- women's rights, minorities, the peace movement," said Marin Supervisor Harry Moore. Moore unsuccessfully ran against Mr. Wornum, as a Democrat for assembly, in 1974. "I 
2 CONC saw him as a real advocate of the people."
2 CONT
2 CONT EXAMINER  Friday, June 16, 1995
2 CONT
2 CONT LARKSPUR - Veteran Marin County politician Michael Wornum - who served in the Assembly, on the Coastal Commission, the county Board of Supervisors and as mayor of two different cities - died Thursday of stomach cancer. He was 70.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wornum was an outspoken politician who, in later years, became as well known for his dogs as for his role in guiding the development of Marin County over three decades. In 1988, Mr. Wornum's wife, Sandra Wornum, attracted medi
2 CONC a attention when neighbors complained about the $15,000 doghouse - complete with four bedrooms, a tiled bathtub and TV set - she had constructed for her 10 Saluki pooches.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wornum - a professional architect as well as painter and sculptor - had designed the canine palace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in London, Mr. Wornum emigrated to California in his youth and began his political career in 1960 with election to the Mill Valley City Council. He served for eight years and was mayor twice. In 1968, Mr. Wornum, a Democrat, 
2 CONC was elected to the county Board of Supervisors. After six years, he won a seat in the Assembly. Mr. Wornum was re-elected in 1976, then defeated in 1978 by Republican William Filante.
2 CONT
2 CONT By then, Mr. Wornum had moved from Mill Valley to Larkspur, where he joined the City Council in 1980 and later became mayor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later, he was appointed to the California Coastal Commission, eventually assuming the chairman's post. Mr. Wornum took a pro-environmental stand on the commission, opposing a Bush administration plan to permit more oil d
2 CONC rilling in coastal waters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yet Mr. Wornum didn't always take a predictable tack. When anti-growth elements set out to defeat a 1991 proposition to provide more water to a parched Marin, Mr. Wornum worked actively on the measure's behalf. It was defeated non
2 CONC etheless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wornum remained involved in local politics until the end, attending meetings as late as a month before he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wornum held a bachelor's degree in architecture and a master's degree in city planning from UC-Berkeley.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by his wife; daughters Amanda Mallory, of Larkspur, and Claudia Wornum, of San Francisco; sons Christopher Wornum, of Alameda, and George Wornum, of Portland; sister Mrs. Alan Fletcher of Sussex, England; stepson Ed
2 CONC ward Dorsaneo, of Cotati; stepdaughter Michelle Dorsaneo, of San Rafael; and former wife Barbara Wornum, of Mill Valley.
2 CONT
2 CONT A memorial service has been set for Sunday at 1 p.m. at the family home, 570 Riviera Circle, Larkspur. The family has asked that memorial contributions be made to the Hospice of Marin.&lt;
