1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Canberra Times HEIRESS FEAREDDROWNED IN KETCH WRECK             SYDNEY, Tuesday.12 July 1950 A wealthy 23-year-old English heiress, Jenefer Wornum, is believed to have drowned when heavy seas washed her luxury 35-f
2 CONC t. ketch on to rocks at Wottamolla, National Park, last night. Only other person on board the ketch, 21-years-old Finnish sea-man, Carl Teppo, survived a night of terror to give news of the wreck.  He climbed a 150 feet cliff and 
2 CONC tramped 16 miles in bare feet through driving rain to the nearest habitation. Teppo said a big wave washed him across the deck and into the sea.  As he went overboard he heard Miss Wornum scream and callout, "Carl, Carl, oh, my fo
2 CONC ot."  “I think she must have her foot tangled in some ropes' Teppo said.  Teppo said he had been washed against some rocks but could not climb up, so had swum until he found one he could climb.  At the top of the cliff Teppo found
2 CONC  a track, and followed it to a week-ender at Warumbul, where the Minister for Local Government, Mr. Cahill, was holidaying.  At dawn to-day, soaking wet, blue with cold and his feet bleeding, Teppo staggered into the week-ender an
2 CONC d in broken English stammered out his story. He then collapsed. Sutherland police believe that Miss Wornum may have been swept under a rocky ledge and her body entangled among rope and wreckage from the ketch. Late today Sgt. Harr
2 CONC y Ware, of the police cliff rescue squad, was lowered 150 feet, and spent an hour and a half examining the ketch wreckage, which was scattered along 100 yards of rocks. Jenefer Wornum is the daughter of a wealthy English architect
2 CONC , Mr. Gray Wornum, of St,John's Wood, London, "and Miriam Wornum, a well-known American interior decorator and author.  The parents are believed to be living in San Francisco. A Bachelor of Science, Miss Wornum arrived in Sydney l
2 CONC ast September to study the habits of Australian marsupial mice at Sydney University.  She bought the 35-f t ketch, “Gentleman’s Agreement," for£1,500 in Sydney last January, and spent another £500 fitting it out.   It was her inte
2 CONC ntion to sail the vessel to England. "The Gentleman's Agreement*, with Miss Wornum and Teppo on board, was lost for 11 days off  the N S.W. coast before being sighted by a trawler off Gabo Island last Friday.  The yacht was taken 
2 CONC in tow by the trawler and brought into Eden.  The vessel was on its way from Eden to Broken Bay when it was wrecked.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sydney Morning Herald 12th July 1950 MISS JENEFER WORNUM Vain Search for Girl near Wrecked Yacht Sydney police will make a second attempt at dawn to-day to find the body of Miss Jenefer Wornum, a 23-year-old London heiress, believ
2 CONC ed to be jammed in the wreckage of luxury yacht deep under-water at Wattamolla, National Park. Yesterday Police Sergeant H. Ware risked his life in a three-hour search of the rocks near the wreckage, below a high cliff, with waves
2 CONC  breaking over his head.  The yacht, the 30ft Gentle-man's Agreement, was wrecked early on Monday night. Onboard with Miss Wornum was a Finnish sailor, Karl Teppo, 21.  Teppo climbed a 240ft cliff, wandered through most of the nig
2 CONC ht barefooted and dazed in National Park, and gave the alarm yesterday morning at Warumbul, 10 miles away at the week-end home of the Deputy Premier, Mr. J. J. Cahill. ERROR IN STORM Miss Wornum and Teppo had made an adventurous j
2 CONC ourney in the yacht from Coffs Harbour, 280 miles north of Sydney.  Teppo said yesterday that their destination had been Sydney. He said they were buffeted by a storm and did not realize they had overshot Sydney until they found l
2 CONC ast week-end they were at Gabo, which is south of the Victorian border, and270 miles beyond Sydney.  They were sailing north for Sydney when the yacht crashed into the rocks in the darkness. Miss Wornum's father is a wealthy Londo
2 CONC n architect, George Grey Wornum, 63, who was in charge of the decorating of the trans-Atlantic liner Queen Elizabeth. Her mother is an authoress. Miss Wornum came to Australia last October to continue research on herpetology (the 
2 CONC study of reptiles) at Sydney University.  She had just graduated in Science in London, and intended taking her Doctorate of Philosophy here. She was especially interested in the study of bats. She bought the ketch Gentle-man's Agr
2 CONC eement in Sydney and had it lavishly refitted.  Miss Wornum wanted to be the first woman skipper in the Sydney-Hobart race, held annually in the Christmas holidays.  She sailed Gentleman's Agreement to Coffs Harbour, arriving on J
2 CONC une 2, with Miss Cynthia Sutton, of Pacific Highway, Gordon, and Mr. Hans Erikson, who lives in his launch moored at Rose Bay. BIRTHDAY DECISION Miss Sutton said yesterday:"On June 15 we celebrated Jenefer's birthday on the yacht.
2 CONC  She then said she wanted to sail it back to Rose Bay to add to her knowledge of yachts.  "Hans Erikson and I returned to Sydney by plane after Karl Teppo, who had served on sailing ships overseas, was engaged to assist her to han
2 CONC dle the yacht."  Teppo, of Bay Street, Double Bay, came from Finland two years ago. He arrived in Coffs Harbour last month as one of the crew of the coastal ship Comaro.  Miss Wornum and Teppo sailed for Sydney a fortnight ago yes
2 CONC terday. Yesterday morning, soon after the first news that the yacht had been wrecked, the Sydney Police Cliff Rescue Squad was called by police radio from a demonstration the members were giving on the cliffs at Watson's Bay under
2 CONC  Sergeant Ware. NEAR COLLAPSE. When Constable K. Heys, of Kogarah, was helping to erect the gear the edge of the cliff crumbled and other police just saved him. He was hauled back by ropes. Sergeant Ware was lowered down the cliff
2 CONC . With a rope tied round his body he searched for three hours among debris from the yacht while waves broke over his head.  He thought he saw the yacht 15ft under water.  After Inspector Hughes signaled him to end the search he wa
2 CONC s hauled up the cliff bruised and on the point of collapse.  He will resume the search at low tide this morning.
