1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Mort Fleishhacker 1932-2011 Mortimer Fleishhacker III, always known to his family as "Mort," passed away peacefully at Davies Medical Center on October 25 from complications resulting from a series of strokes. He was 79 years old.
2 CONC  Mort was born on September 5, 1932, the eldest son of Mortimer Fleishhacker. Jr. and Janet Choynski Fleishhacker. His family has been part of San Francisco since his great-grandfather, Aaron, a pioneer in the development of land 
2 CONC and mining in Nevada, arrived here in 1851. After graduating from Princeton in 1954 with a degree in Chemical Engineering, Mort served in the army and subsequently received his MBA from U.C. Berkeley in 1957, after which he was in
2 CONC volved in a variety of business ventures and entrepreneurial activities including serving on the board of directors of Natomas Corporation and American President Lines. However Mort's primary interest centered upon serving the com
2 CONC munity in the tradition of his family. He was a member of the Fleishhacker Foundation Board for many decades. He served as President of Temple Emanuel (a role his father once held) from 1987 to 1990, during which time he led the c
2 CONC ampaign to rebuild its historic structure. Additional organizations he led or served with passion and zeal included SPUR, A.C.T., the Greenbelt Alliance, the San Francisco Jewish Family Services Agency, The Big Brothers, the Guard
2 CONC smen and The San Francisco Development Fund. In the past few years he was instrumental in securing historical recognition for and assisting the Garden Conservancy in the preservation of "Green Gables," the family estate in Woodsid
2 CONC e which dates back to 1911. Mort and Frannie, his beloved wife of 56 years, have shown strong commitments to numerous local charities and organizations, and their generous but quiet support is well known. Frequent global travelers
2 CONC , they found time for extensive voyages to all corners of the world. An avid and accomplished skier, Mort could regularly be seen at Sugar Bowl, which Mort had enjoyed since its founding in 1939. He was a member of the French Club
2 CONC , a lover of fine wine, and an energetic tennis player, hiker, and bicyclist. Mort is survived by his wife, Francoise, (Frannie), his three children: Leslie Gaylord of Long Beach, Sandi Randall of Denver, Marc Fleishhacker of Shor
2 CONC t Hills, New Jersey, and eight grandchildren. His sister, Delia Ehrlich, and brother, David Fleishhacker, still reside in San Francisco and Woodside. Friends are welcome to attend a celebration of his life to be held at the Americ
2 CONC an Conservatory Theater, 415 Geary St., on Friday, October 28 at 11 a.m. The family kindly requests, in lieu of flowers, that donations be made to A.C.T., smazow@act-sf.org or the Greenbelt Alliance info@greenbelt.org Logo Publish
2 CONC ed in San Francisco Chronicle from Oct. 26 to Oct. 27, 2011 
