1 OCCU Silk Merchant G.K.Mayer (Spiegelgasse 15, Vienna
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Converted to Lutheran Christianity (helvetic confession), baptised September 8th, 1830 (age 23), at which point he officially changed his name to Gabriel Mayer. He still left "Kohn" in the name of his company thoug
2 CONC h and sometimes used "Gabriel K. Mayer" or "G.K. Mayer". (www.GenTeam.at; Converts in Vienna; no. 5006, 5007)
2 CONT
2 CONT His children didn't use the "Kohn" name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from Christof Kuhn, third great grandson, who manages: https://gw.geneanet.org/kuhni74_f?lang=en&p=salomon&n=kohn
2 CONT
2 CONT A change to Bavaria’s constitution required Jews to take surnames officially only in 1818. Previously, Jews could and did use their father's names as a patronymic surname, including Moses Mayer Kohn, the father of Salomon/Gabriel.
2 CONC  Of the four known sons of Moses, two took the surname Mayer, their father's patronym via his own father Meir, and two took Kohn (although Nathan Kohn is frequently called Kohn-Mayer in the baptisms of his grandchildren). Gabriel 
2 CONC himself was initially baptized under Mayer and legally changed his surname to Kohn-Mayer in 1837 to reflect his “sprünglicher und eigentlicher" name, as found in his baptismal register notation. Sons of Gabriel that used the surna
2 CONC me Kohn include Ferdinand Eduard Kohn-Mayer as seen at his 1876 marriage recorded in the Jewish Eheaufgebotsbuch. 
