1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.sfgate.com/news/article/Madeleine-Haas-Russell-2937726.php:
2 CONT
2 CONT Madeleine Haas Russell, one of San Francisco's most revered philanthropists and civic leaders, died at her home Friday -- her 84th birthday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Russell, a great-grandniece of Levi Strauss, was intensely active in behalf of causes ranging from the abolition of the death penalty to human rights advocacy and prevention of war, as well as one of the city's most generous 
2 CONC supporters of culture and the arts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although raised in a Republican family, Mrs. Russell was an avid advocate of Democratic Party policies.
2 CONT
2 CONT She took great pride in her appointment by President John F. Kennedy to be director of the U.S. State Department Reception Center in San Francisco, a position she held for seven years, and of the support she gave over the years to
2 CONC  candidates in mayoral, senatorial and presidential races.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mrs. Russell has made her mark as a citizen, humanitarian and philanthropist," Hillary Rodham Clinton said yesterday. "We all owe her a debt of gratitude for making our country a better place."
2 CONT
2 CONT A fourth-generation San Franciscan, Madeleine Haas was born in 1915 and orphaned at the age of 12 upon the death of her father, Charles Haas, head of the San Francisco business firm of Haas Brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT She attended Smith College, graduating magna cum laude with a bachelor of arts degree in 1937. In 1946, she married Leon B. Russell, a screenwriter for Metro-Goldwyn- Mayer and later a Pan American World Airways executive. The cou
2 CONC ple were divorced in 1959.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heiress to large fortunes from both sides of her family, Mrs. Russell began her philanthropic work almost as soon as she came of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT An independent thinker whose ideas often clashed with popular consensus or current fashion, Mrs. Russell and her brother William established the Columbia Foundation in 1940. One of California's earliest private foundations, it con
2 CONC tinues to support a wide range of environmental, cultural and social causes.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the foundation's first grants was given to the Japanese American Student Relocation Council, making it possible for students forced into relocation camps during World War II to continue their studies at universities in East
2 CONC ern states.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another early award recipient was the Emergency Committee of Atomic Scientists to help discourage the further use of nuclear weapons and energy, a donation that was gratefully acknowledged in a note from Albert Einstein, the group
2 CONC 's founding chairman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Russell's maverick spirit was also reflected in her home -- nicknamed "Fort Russell" by the neighbors, who were scandalized when she razed her grandfather's brick mansion in Presidio Heights and replaced it in 1950 with a Bau
2 CONC haus- style structure designed by architect Erich Mendelsohn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Up to the time of her death, she was on the boards of the Hebrew University of Jerusalem, the Harry S. Truman Institute for the Advancement of Peace (of which she was a founding director), the Asia Foundation, the San Francisco Op
2 CONC era Association, Brandeis University, the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art and the Columbia Foundation.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had also served on the boards of KQED (where she served as chairwoman), the Jewish Community Endowment Fund and the Levi Strauss Foundation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Russell's strong connection to the Jewish community was marked by her early and steadfast support for the State of Israel, as well as for local Jewish community institutions, including the Jewish Community Federation and Cong
2 CONC regation Emanu-El.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the earliest to inveigh against the fallibility of agricultural systems dependent on chemicals, Mrs. Russell funded the first laboratory in the University of California system devoted to growing produce sustainably and organ
2 CONC ically.
2 CONT
2 CONT As president of the Columbia Foundation, Mrs. Russell directed many awards toward groups focused on protecting human rights, including support for abolition of the death penalty, for the normalization of attitudes toward homosexua
2 CONC lity and for the right to die with dignity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Russell is survived by her three children and their families: Alice and Bill Russell-Shapiro and their children, Maddy and Will, all of San Francisco; Christine H. Russell and Mark Schlesinger and their children, Ben and Char
2 CONC lie, also of San Francisco; and Charles P. Russell and Soujata Devaris, and their son, Alasdair, of London.
2 CONT
2 CONT A public memorial will be held tomorrow at 3:30 p.m. at Temple Emanu-El, Arguello Boulevard and Lake Street, in San Francisco.
2 CONT
2 CONT Memorial donations may be made to any of the charities that Mrs. Russell favored.
2 CONT
2 CONT April 7, 1999
2 CONT By J.L. Pimsleur
