1 NAME Theodore Seixas /Solomons/
2 GIVN Theodore Seixas
2 SURN Solomons
2 _MARNM Solomons
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 27 JUL 1870
2 ADDR
3 CITY San Francisco
3 STAE California
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 26 MAY 1947
2 ADDR
3 CITY Los Angeles
3 STAE California
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Mountaineer, Cartographer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Theodore was known as the pioneer of the John Muir Trail.  He was the first to photograph the Tuolumne Canyon and such summits as Banner Peak and Mount Ritter. He discovered, and named, the great peaks of the Evolu
2 CONC tion Range. Most importantly, he had the vision for, and surveyed and mapped the major portion of the high mountain route that was to become the famed John Muir Trail of the High Sierra. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a mountain named for him, Mt. Solomons, in Fresno County, California, (13016 ft.), according to Peter Browning, Place Names of the Sierra Nevada, in http://www.snwburd.com/bob/etymology/solomons.html.  See also the review a
2 CONC rticle by Richard H. Dillon of Shirley Sargent's book, Solomons of the Sierra, in http://www.greatwestbooks.com/solomons.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_Solomons
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodore Seixas Solomons (1870–1947) was an explorer and early member of the Sierra Club. From 1892 to 1897 he explored and named the Mount Goddard, Evolution Valley and Evolution Basin region in what is now northern Kings Canyon 
2 CONC National Park in eastern California. He was instrumental in envisioning, exploring, and establishing the route of what became the John Muir Trail from Yosemite Valley along the crest of the Sierra Nevada to Mount Whitney.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life and ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in San Francisco, California on July 20, 1870, the second son and the fifth of seven children of Hannah Marks, an influential San Francisco educator and civic worker and Gershom Mendes Seixas Solomons. He had relocated
2 CONC  to San Francisco from New York City during the Gold Rush, and founded Congregation Emanu-El in 1854. He was also the first president of any West Coast lodge of B'nai B'rith. His great-grandfather was Gershom Mendes Seixas (1745–1
2 CONC 816), the "Patriot Rabbi", the first native-born Rabbi in the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Solomons later recalled that the idea that resulted in the John Muir Trail originated in his adolescence. "The idea of a crest-parallel trail came to me one day while herding my uncle's cattle in an immense unfenced alfalfa field 
2 CONC near Fresno. It was 1884 and I was 14." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and family
2 CONT
2 CONT Solomons married three times. He married as his first wife, on March 29, 1901, at Dawson Creek, British Columbia, Canada, Rozella M. Gould of Dawson Creek. They were later divorced. There were no children from this marriage. He ma
2 CONC rried on January 8, 1909, in New York City, as his second wife, Katherine Gray Church, born on May 6, 1881 in New York City the only daughter of Henry Seymour Church and Margaretta Josephine Gray. She died on February 7, 1971 in C
2 CONC herryland, Alameda County, California. Her mother, a published writer and singer, was born into a family with deep New England roots that trace back to the Rev. Mr. Blackleach Burritt, and Governor Thomas Welles. After his second 
2 CONC wife was committed to a mental institution, he married Yvonne Robinson who died in 1965. They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodore and Katherine were the parents of three children: Eleanor Susan Brownell Anthony "Toni" Solomons (1911–2006), David Seixas Solomons (1913–1961), and Leon Henry Solomons (1915–1988).
2 CONT
2 CONT They lived at a house he named the Flying Spur, which he build on 20 acres (81,000 m2) of land that juts out over the Merced River Canyon. It is located at 4,600 feet (1,400 m) in the Stanislaus National Forest adjacent to Yosemit
2 CONC e National Park.
2 CONT
2 CONT Explorations
2 CONT
2 CONT In his explorations, Solomons correctly determined the courses of the upper branches of the San Joaquin River. In 1892, accompanied by Joseph Nisbet LeConte and Sidney I. Peixotto, he crossed from Mount Lyell by way of Rush Creek 
2 CONC to the base of Mount Ritter and ascended the peak. In 1895, Solomons took his most notable trip, accompanied by Ernest C. Bonner. Ascending the South Fork of the San Joaquin they came to the group of mountains now designated the E
2 CONC volution Group, named by Solomons. The highest of these he called Mount Darwin (after the evolutionist Charles Darwin), and the others he named Haeckel, Wallace, Fiske, Spencer, and Huxley, after famous evolutionists of the day. C
2 CONC ontinuing their explorations, Solomons and Bonner ascended Mount Goddard, then made their way down to Simpson Meadow via North Goddard Creek, and were the first to make this section known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Solomons’ excursions in the next two years added details to the knowledge of Sierra topography, but his principal contribution was an accurate map which he drafted and presented to the Sierra Club in 1896.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and memorials
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Los Angeles, California on May 27, 1947. Mount Solomons (13016') is named after him.
