1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Hahn, Joachim: Jüdisches Leben in Esslingen. Geschichte, Quellen und Dokumentation, pages 371 to 379. Partial translation:
2 CONT > Abraham Leopold, who after 1812 assumed the family name "Eßlinger", was one of the first Jews from Wankheim, who were accepted in Esslingen by a protection letter of 1806 (see p. 23). He was born on March 4, 1776, in Dettensee, 
2 CONC the son of Leopold (Abraham, later Leopold Eßlinger), and Esther was born to Loew. He was married to Fanny (Friederike), Lazarus, on June 15, 1802. She was born as the daughter of Isaac Lazarus and Sara b. Ochs (see p.287f.) On 2 
2 CONC or 10 April 1786 (place of birth unknown). The married couple had seven children: 1. Moritz (Moses), born 15 October 1802 (see p. 372 f.): 2. Joseph, born 17 December 1805, later resided in Esslingen, May 1832 was incorporated int
2 CONC o the civil right °; He was named as a gold worker in WV 1835 and the owner of a jeweler factory in 1837 (see p.55) and was buried "in silence" on the Jewish cemetery in Wankheim on January 14, 1840, after he had left in Rottenbur
2 CONC g / In prison; 3. Nanette, born 30 September 1807 in Esslingen, died as a child in Rottweil; 4. Friderica, born 23 December 1808 in Esslingen, died as a child in Rottweil; 5. Pauline, born 25 December 1811 in Esslingen, died as a 
2 CONC child in Rottweil; 6. Ernestine, born December 3, 1812 in Esslingen, died as a child in Rottweil; 7. Henriette, born January 4, 1816 in Rottweil, emigrated to Sigmaringen in 1845. "
2 CONT
2 CONT >At the end of 1810, Abraham Leopold had already begged the royal authorities to be allowed to settle in Rottweil, since he had been trading mainly in the Villingen area for 22 years. The magistrate of Rottweil spoke out against h
2 CONC is admission. He also received a rejection from Rottweil on August 22, 1812: "The town is overrun with commerce, and the Jews make the artisans and businessmen sensitive by their peddlers, and it is not probable that The petitione
2 CONC r would not fulfill his promise to do any action here; rather, let his growing family fear that in a short time the Jewish state would be spread even further, and that a delicate thrust would be brought to the local market; That t
2 CONC he police should still be so strictly guarded over the secret peddlers, but the daily experience teaches that the Jews know how to avoid all precaution. " Already in connection with this admission, Abraham Leopold calls himself th
2 CONC e family name »Eßlinger«. On January 21, 1813, Abraham again presented his request to the royal authorities. He was able to point out that he had already purchased the former Rottweiler Cameral apartment at 4400 Gulden. He was the
2 CONC n allowed to settle in Rottweil by Royal Decree of 6 June 1813.
2 CONT Already on March 21, 1817, Abraham Eßlinger died in Rottweil. The four daughters Nanette, Friderica, Pauline and Ernestine died within one month (year unknown) of a nervous fever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mentioned in the book Portraits of Our Past: Jews of the German Countryside. https://books.google.com/books?id=N9KbwXcS7OgC According to the book, Abraham was born in Dettensee and had lived in Wankheim before applying to get prot
2 CONC ection in Rottweil, and he "adopted the name of his wife's hometown, Esslingen, as his family name."
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.landesarchiv-bw.de/plink/?f=2-2414150
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.freunde-juedischer-kultur-esslingen.de/historie2.htm
2 CONT >Leopold Abraham [1776 Dettensee - 1817 Rottweil], who bore the family name Esslinger from 1812, married Fanny [Friederike] Lazarus [1786 - her death date and her tomb are unknown]. They came with two small sons to Esslingen, wher
2 CONC e they were still five daughters, four of them already dying in the childhood - all with a "nervous fever" - in 1810 the family moved to Rottweil.
