1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Anita Seligman Lothar Alexander, a member of a prominent Jewish family who had roots deep in both San Francisco and prewar Germany, died Tuesday at her home in Oakland of the infirmities of old age. Mrs. Alexander 
2 CONC was 97.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a survivor of the Nazi Holocaust and escaped from Europe only after a series of harrowing encounters with German military forces. She then moved to the Bay Area and was active as a teacher well into her 90s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Alexander was born Anita Seligman in Frankfurt am Main in 1904; her father was a judge and her grandfather founded Casella and Co. a dye-making firm that was one of the I.G. Farben companies. She attended the University of Fr
2 CONC ankfurt and was one of the first female students at the University of Seville in Spain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT LATEST SFGATE VIDEOS
2 CONT
2 CONT     Now Playing
2 CONT     Pod of orcas seen playing in Monterey Bay... Randy Straka / Princess Monterey Whale Watching
2 CONT     Cupid Undie Run in San Francisco... Douglas Zimmerman, SFGate
2 CONT     Nude Valentine's Day Parade in SF... Douglas Zimmerman, SFGate
2 CONT     Mt. Olive Organic Farm in Paso Robles... San Francisco Chronicle
2 CONT     Fostering Failure... San Francisco Chronicle
2 CONT     SF firefighters rescue 2 dogs that fell off cliff... Twitter / @sffdpio
2 CONT     Ryan Coogler in Oakland for Black Panther premiere... Peter Hartlaub, The Chronicle
2 CONT     Good Samaritans pull man from burning car on I-80... CHP Contra Costa
2 CONT     Coyotes seen hunting in San Francisco... Andrew Bland
2 CONT     Weather that causes dry spells in the Bay Area... Ted Andersen, Alix Martichoux, SFGATE 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1931, she married Hans Lothar, publisher of Die Frankfurter Zeitung, a Jewish-owned publication considered to be the leading liberal paper in Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nazis came to power in 1933 and in 1936 seized the paper.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just before this happened, Lothar was reassigned to the paper's London bureau. It was a subterfuge to get him out of the country; the Frankfurtur Zeitung had no London bureau, but the paper's owners had discovered that Lothar was 
2 CONC on a Gestapo list to be arrested.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Lothar returned to Germany and persuaded her own family, which had lived in Germany for generations, that they were no longer safe. They moved to Switzerland. Mrs. Lothar and her two young children joined them in Switzerland.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1941, Lothar himself was in Britain as publisher of a British-sponsored newspaper for expatriate Germans. He became concerned that his family was not safe even in neutral Switzerland, which was surrounded by German-controlled t
2 CONC erritory.
2 CONT
2 CONT He arranged passage on a train from Geneva through Vichy France. Mrs. Lothar and her two children, Nicholas and Corinna, were to ride in a "sealed" car. The Germans had agreed not to disturb passengers on this car. However, it was
2 CONC  a train ride though the heart of occupied Europe. Armed troops were at all stations and Nazi agents went though the car with a list of persons; some were taken away, never to be seen again.
2 CONT
2 CONT The German authorities had a list of everyone on the train but somehow did not connect Mrs. Lothar and her children with her husband, who was a wanted man in Nazi Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the train arrived at the Spanish frontier, Mrs. Lothar bribed a bus driver to get a place on a bus bound for Barcelona and safety. They eventually traveled to Lisbon and then took a ship to the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, U.S. authorities would not let them dock. Their travel documents were not in order. "My mother was an intelligent and persuasive woman," said Corinna Lothar Metcalf, who was on the trip, "and so we were admitted to this c
2 CONC ountry."
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Lothar and her children came to the Bay Area to rejoin another branch of the Seligman family. Her grandfather, Henry Seligman, had come to California in the mid-19th century Gold Rush and had founded an investment bank. Henry
2 CONC  Seligman was also one of the founders of Temple Emanu-El in San Francisco.
2 CONT
2 CONT The war separated Mrs. Lothar from her husband. She never saw him again. He died in England in 1944.
2 CONT
2 CONT Four years later, Mrs. Lothar married Robert Alexander, a San Francisco physician who was a refugee from Nazi Germany. He died in 1959.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Alexander then earned a master's degree in special education at San Francisco State University and taught remedial reading at Holy Names College in Oakland and at Oakland's Raskob Institute. She also tutored private students.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son, Nicholas Lothar, died in 1964 at the age of 30. "My mother lost two husbands, and lost her country, but the death of her son was the tragedy that affected her most," said her daughter, Corinna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Corinna Lothar Metcalf, who practices law in Washington, D.C., a grandson, and two great-grandchildren survive. There will be no services. 
