1 NAME Richard Madron /Seligman/
2 GIVN Richard Madron
2 _MARNM Seligman
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 10 NOV 1918
2 ADDR
3 CITY Leatherhead
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 JUL 2002
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Home»
2 CONT     News»
2 CONT     Obituaries
2 CONT
2 CONT Madron Seligman
2 CONT
2 CONT 12:01AM BST 12 Jul 2002
2 CONT
2 CONT Madron Seligman, who has died aged 83, was Conservative Member of the European Parliament for West Sussex from 1979 to 1994, but was better known to a wider public as the oldest friend of the former prime minister Sir Edward Heath
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT An open, likable, clubbable man of wide European sympathies, Seligman arrived at Balliol College, Oxford, in 1937, halfway through Edward Heath's time there. Out of their shared interest in music and politics developed a firm and 
2 CONC life-long friendship.
2 CONT
2 CONT In later years, after Seligman's marriage, the Seligmans became almost a substitute family for the bachelor politician. Heath became godfather to Seligman's eldest son, Lincoln, and by all accounts took his avuncular responsibilit
2 CONC ies very seriously.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heath also frequently accompanied the family on holidays abroad, and it was through messing around on a dinghy while staying with the Seligmans at their cottage at St Jacut-de-la-Mer in Brittany, that he acquired his love of saili
2 CONC ng.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seligman shared his friend's belief in the importance of European unity. In the late 1990s, after retiring from the European Parliament, he emerged as a trenchant critic of the Conservative Party's growing resistance to further Eu
2 CONC ropean integration. Related Articles
2 CONT
2 CONT     James Moorhouse 12 Jan 2014
2 CONT
2 CONT     Alan Tyrrell 01 Dec 2014
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Madron Seligman was born at Leatherhead, Surrey, on November 10 1918. The Seligmans were originally a banking family who had settled in Britain and America after leaving Germany in the 19th century. Madron's father preferr
2 CONC ed industry to the City, however, and had founded APV Exports, a Crawley-based company making a wide range of industrial equipment, mainly for food and drink processing.
2 CONT
2 CONT After leaving Harrow, where he played cricket for the school, Seligman went up Balliol to read PPE. He became president of the Union, excelled at cricket, rugby and tennis, and represented the university at skiing. He would later 
2 CONC represent Britain in the sport at the 1952 Winter Olympics in Oslo.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1939 he and Ted Heath took a hiking holiday together in Germany and Poland, an extraordinarily foolhardy venture, especially for Seligman, who was half Jewish. In Warsaw, British embassy officials warned them to get out of Pola
2 CONC nd as quickly as they could. By a mixture of crowded trains and hitch-hiking they got to the border. They were in Leipzig on August 26 when news broke of the Molotov-Ribbentrop pact; they escaped to France just in time to avoid be
2 CONC ing caught in Germany at the outbreak of hostilities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like all close friendships, theirs was punctuated by occasional bouts of exasperation. In the train from Leipzig, Heath recalled in his memoirs: "Madron insisted on pulling out his penny whistle, playing once again the same old tu
2 CONC nes I had heard incessantly for the past three weeks - all slightly out of tune.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I had come to dislike these dirges intensely, and had already lost my temper with him after his umpteenth rendition of Colonel Bogey on the platform at Leipzig station. Now, as we tried to escape from Germany on this ghastly trai
2 CONC n, out came that wretched penny whistle again. It drove me up the wall. We had another flaming row, but at least I won on this occasion and we had some peace afterwards."
2 CONT
2 CONT Their ways parted during the war and, while Heath enlisted in the Royal Artillery, Seligman served with the 6th Armoured Divisional Signals in North Africa and Italy, and fought at Monte Cassino, before being demobbed in the rank 
2 CONC of major.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, Seligman joined the family industrial machinery business. He helped to make the company a success story and rose to be managing director. From 1960 to 1988, he was chairman of the incinerator company Eaton Socon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seligman entered the first directly-elected European Parliament as MEP for West Sussex in 1979, and at the same time made it into the Guinness Book of Records for having the largest majority (95,484) in any European democratic ele
2 CONC ction. He held the seat until his retirement in 1994 and served on the European Parliament's energy and animal welfare committees. A modest and private man, he was greatly liked by his colleagues and played the piano for the Conse
2 CONC rvative Group at Christmas revues.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seligman remained in regular contact with Ted Heath. They sailed together, competed with each other to lose weight, and remained close confidants.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the day Margaret Thatcher was ousted from power by her own cabinet, Heath got the news early and was said to have telephoned Seligman with the simple message: "Rejoice, rejoice!" Although he remained a Conservative, and was a g
2 CONC ood and amusing speaker, Seligman was never very interested in party politics per se. His belief in the importance of the European Union remained the focal point of his political involvement throughout his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1999, he was one of nine former MPs and MEPs who signed a letter to the Times, attacking the Conservative leader William Hague's tough stance on Europe and hinting that they might support the break-away pro-euro Conservatives f
2 CONC or the European Elections that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later, as chairman of Veterans for Europe, Seligman attacked a speech delivered by Lady Thatcher - in which she had suggested that to surrender the pound would "betray all that past generations down the ages lived and di
2 CONC ed to defend".
2 CONT
2 CONT Seligman described the speech as an "insult to the memory of those millions who gave their lives for peace and freedom in Europe in the Second World War". The lesson of history, he maintained, was the need for greater, not less, c
2 CONC o-operation in Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seligman was a very kind man, who always saw the best in people. Though he differed with many of his fellow Conservatives over Europe, he never made any enemies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seligman, who was appointed CBE in 1994, died on Tuesday.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, in 1947, Nancy-Joan Marks, who survives him, together with their three sons and a daughter.
