1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Richard N. Goldman (April 16, 1920 – November 29, 2010) was an American philanthropist who co-founded the Goldman Environmental Prize in 1990 with his wife, Rhoda Goldman.The Jewish Telegraphic Agency has called Go
2 CONC ldman "one of the most influential Jewish philanthropists in the United States." He founded the insurance company Goldman Insurance and Risk Management, and with his wife he established the Richard and Rhoda Goldman Fund in 1951.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard and Rhoda Goldman established the Goldman Environmental Prize in San Francisco, California, in 1990.Goldman's foundation, which is sometimes nicknamed the "Green Nobel," awarded six prizes annually worth $150,000 USD to en
2 CONC vironmental activists representing six regions of the world. Approximately $13.2 million has been awarded to activists from more than 70 countries since the Goldmans established the award, as of 2010. The 1991 Goldman Environment 
2 CONC Prize winner Wangari Maathai from Kenya and founder of the Green Belt Movement, was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to his work with the Goldman Environmental Prize, Goldman supported beautification projects in San Francisco, and co-founded the Richard and Rhoda Goldman Fund. Through his foundation, which is worth more than one bill
2 CONC ion dollars, Goldman funded projects throughout the San Francisco Bay Area, including the Jewish Community Center of San Francisco and the Rhoda Goldman Plaza. Among his California projects were investments in solar power, and pro
2 CONC tection of redwood forests and sea life. In 2004, he was awarded the Chairman's Medal in the 11th Annual Heinz Award. The Richard and Rhoda Goldman School of Public Policy at UC Berkeley is named for the Goldmans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life Richard Goldman was the son of lawyer Richard Samuel Goldman and his wife Alice Wertheim Goldman. He died at his home in San Francisco on November 29, 2010, at the age of 90. He was survived by his daughter, Susan Ge
2 CONC lman, and two sons, John and Doug. He was predeceased by his wife, philanthropist Rhoda Haas Goldman, who died in 1996 and their son, Richard, who died in 1989. A memorial service was held at Congregation Emanu-El in San Francisco
2 CONC . From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Goldman 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Richard N. Goldman was an American philanthropist who co-founded the Goldman Environmental Prize in 1990 with his wife, Rhoda Goldman. The Jewish Telegraphic Agency has called Goldman "one of the most influential Jewish philanthro
2 CONC pists in the United States."  He founded the insurance company Goldman Insurance and Risk Management, and with his wife he established the Richard and Rhoda Goldman Fund in 1951.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard and Rhoda Goldman established the Goldman Environmental Prize in San Francisco, California, in 1990. Goldman's foundation, which is sometimes nicknamed the "Green Nobel," awarded six prizes annually worth $150,000 USD to e
2 CONC nvironmental activists representing six regions of the world. Approximately $13.2 million has been awarded to activists from more than 70 countries since the Goldmans established the award, as of 2010. The 1991 Goldman Environment
2 CONC  Prize winner Wangari Maathai from Kenya and founder of the Green Belt Movement, was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to his work with the Goldman Environmental Prize, Goldman supported beautification projects in San Francisco, and co-founded the Richard and Rhoda Goldman Fund. Through his foundation, which is worth more than one bill
2 CONC ion dollars, Goldman funded projects throughout the San Francisco Bay Area, including the Jewish Community Center of San Francisco and the Rhoda Goldman Plaza. Among his California projects were investments in solar power, and pro
2 CONC tection of redwood forests and sealife. In 2004, he was awarded the Chairman's Medal in the 11th Annual Heinz Award. The Richard and Rhoda Goldman School of Public Policy at UC Berkeley is named for the Goldmans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Goldman was the son of lawyer Richard Samuel Goldman and his wife Alice Wertheim Goldman. He died at his home in San Francisco on November 29, 2010, at the age of 90. He was survived by his daughter, Susan Gelman, and two 
2 CONC sons, John and Doug. He was predeceased by his wife, philanthropist Rhoda Haas Goldman, who died in 1996 and their son, Richard, who died in 1989. A memorial service was held at Congregation Emanu-El in San Francisco.
