1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Opera Soprano in New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT New York Times, 1968
2 CONT
2 CONT GLADYS TAYLER, 72; GAVE OPERA PRIZES
2 CONT
2 CONT Gladys Axman Tayler, former opera soprano, died at Memorial Hospital on Tuesday night following a brief illness. She was 72 years old and the wife of William Tayler.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Tayler, the daughter of August Weil of Boston, studied in New York with Estelle Liebling and other private teachers. As Gladys Axman (her first husband was Clarence Axman), she made her debut with the Metropolitan Opera on De
2 CONC c. 27, 1919, as Joy of Understanding in the world premiere of "The Blue Bird." Albert Wolff's operatic version of the Maeterlinck play.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she ended her singing career in the late nineteen-thirties, she became a patron of musical organizations. In 1964 she established an annual $2,000 award, bearing her name, to be given at the Metropolitan Opera's auditions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage to Mr. Axman ended in divorce. She is survived by her second husband.
