1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/23356233/person/112428039318 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 27 2022, 12:36:47 UTC''
2 CONT S.F. BUSINESSMAN, BENEFACTOR PHILIP NAUMBURG DIES AT 75: Newspaper Obituary and Death NoticeSanta Fe New Mexican, The (NM) - Wednesday, May 31, 1995Deceased Name: S.F. BUSINESSMAN, BENEFACTOR PHILIP NAUMBURG DIES AT 75
2 CONT Longtime Santa Fe businessman and community benefactor Philip H. Naumburg, died Monday morning at his home after an almost four-year struggle with cancer. He was 75. Naumburg, an heir of the wealthy Morgenthau family, moved to San
2 CONC ta Fe from Pennsylvania in 1960 and founded Colony Materials with a handful of employees. The business, off West Airport Road, now employs 85 people and supplies customers in Santa Fe and Albuquerque with building materials. Naumb
2 CONC urg was known for his generosity to his employees, many of whom have been with the company for years. In a two-page advertisement in the April 20 edition of The New Mexican , paid for from their own pockets, those employees thanke
2 CONC d their boss for ''his great sense of humor, integrity, honesty, generosity and his great foresight.'' The company's office manager, Mary Lou Shaw, has worked for Colony for 17 years. ''He was the best thing that ever happened to 
2 CONC me,'' she said. ''He was like a second father to me.'' Friends and acquaintances said Naumburg's death was a personal loss that would be felt in the community at large. ''He was active in almost everything that goes on in Santa Fe
2 CONC ,'' said retired Gen. Jack Juhan, a longtime friend of Naumburg's. ''He was one of the best citizens Santa Fe has ever had.'' Juhan said Naumburg and his company especially supported the United Way of Santa Fe County. Ike Kalangis
2 CONC , a Santa Fe banker who is chairman, president and CEO of Boatmen's Southwest, the parent company of Sunwest banks, called Naumburg's death ''a huge loss. Phil has done so much for the community. I call him the godfather of the Un
2 CONC ited Way.'' Naumburg's dedication and commitment to the community and to his employees at Colony Materials ''will be a legacy for years to come,'' Kalangis said. Naumburg had been a board member of the United Way, St. Vincent Hosp
2 CONC ital, the Chamber Music Festival and The College of Santa Fe. ''I've known him since he first moved to Santa Fe,'' said Harvey Chalker, owner of Alpine Sports. ''Phil was probably the kindest, most generous man I ever met in my li
2 CONC fe.'' Stan Davis, founder of the contracting firm Davis and Associates, was also a friend of Naumburg's. ''He is a person we're going to miss,'' Davis said. ''He was a very good man for Santa Fe. He did a lot of things people didn
2 CONC 't really know about. He was an outstanding citizen.'' Davis said Naumburg had been involved in a variety of community activities since he moved to Santa Fe 35 years ago. Philip Naumburg was born in New York City, the son of Georg
2 CONC e Naumburg and Ruth Morgenthau, who was a member of the wealthy Morgenthau family and the daughter of Henry Morgenthau Jr., President Franklin Roosevelt's secretary of the treasury from 1934 to 1945. He attended school in New York
2 CONC  and prep school in Deerfield, Mass., before entering Ohio State University, where he graduated in 1942 with a bachelor's degree in agriculture. He later joined the Army as a second lieutenant and served in Italy and France during
2 CONC  World War II. Naumburg had farms in Ireland and near Doylestown, Pa., before he moved to Santa Fe to start Colony Materials. The name was derived from the names of the state where his mother-in-law lived, Colorado, and from his n
2 CONC ative state, New York. Naumburg is survived by his wife, Odette, and by children, Stephen, Judith Naumburg, and Philip Jr., his step-daughter Aline Roth, as well many other relatives. A memorial service is scheduled for 2 p.m. Fri
2 CONC day at St. Francis Auditorium at the Museum of Fine Arts, 107 W. Palace Ave. That will be followed by a reception at Colony Materials on West Airport Road. Instead of flowers, the family has requested donations be made to The Hosp
2 CONC ice Center, Santa Fe Chamber Music Festival, Santa Fe Community Foundation or the Cancer Research Center.
