1 NOTE Sophie and Adolph were hidden in Holland from the Nazis and escaped successfully, according to grandson Lawrence Fried.
2 CONT Here are notes on Sophie from grandson Lawrence Fried:
2 CONT A wonderful grandmother and a terrific knitter (among other talents). She and her husband Adolf were hidden - with Sophie's sister Ida -  for 19 months from the Nazis in an attic in Holland; then came over to San Francisco on a Su
2 CONC nday in Nov. 1946, where they lived in the same house as Hanna and William Fried until their deaths. According to a letter by William Fried, they were hidden by Catholic nuns.  There exists also a letter written by Sophie shortly 
2 CONC after their liberation (Oct. 1944), and translated by William, describing their experiences. There is another letter written in 1945, plus one written in 1939. There is a copy of an inquiry sent through the Red Cross from Hanna an
2 CONC d Joe, dated March 11, 1943 to which "Mutter, Vater, Ida" responded on 1 Jan 1945. For some reason it was stamped by the Red Cross Inquiry Unit on Jul 2, 1945, which seems rather late, because I think we knew their address when I 
2 CONC was still in Europe. (Incidentally, Larry left aboard a troopship for Europe just after midnight, the very same New Year's eve).  According to Sophie's Declaration of Intention, they entered the US on Oct. 22, 1946, at N.Y., arriv
2 CONC ing on plane Nr. NC-90903.
