1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Reserve Infantry Regiment 76  came from Hamburg and was part of 17th Reserve Division.
2 CONT
2 CONT The regimental history states that all its members came from Schleswig Holstein, Mecklenburg and aus den Hansestaedten [from the Hanseatic towns], which means in practical terms Hamburg (hence, possibly, the RIR 76 reference) and 
2 CONC Lubeck.
2 CONT
2 CONT I note that in the Marx family album there is a photograph of a little girl on a swing, and the photographer's mark is from Hamburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT 17th Reserve Division (German Empire) From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 17th Reserve Division (17. Reserve-Division) Active 	1914-1919 Country 	Germany Branch 	Army Type 	Infantry Size 	Approx. 15,000 Engagements 	World War I:
2 CONC  Battle of the Somme, Battle of Arras (1917), Passchendaele, Battle of Cambrai (1918)
2 CONT
2 CONT The 17th Reserve Division (17. Reserve-Division) was a unit of the Imperial German Army in World War I. The division was formed on mobilization of the German Army in August 1914.[1] The division was disbanded in 1919 during the de
2 CONC mobilization of the German Army after World War I. At the beginning of the war, it formed the IX Reserve Corps with the 18th Reserve Division. Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT     1 Recruitment
2 CONT     2 Combat chronicle
2 CONT     3 Order of battle on mobilization
2 CONT     4 Order of battle on March 28, 1918
2 CONT     5 References
2 CONT     6 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Recruitment
2 CONT
2 CONT The division was composed primarily of troops from the Free and Hanseatic Cities and from Schleswig-Holstein. The division included one regular infantry brigade, the 81st, raised in Schleswig-Holstein and Lübeck, and one reserve i
2 CONC nfantry brigade, the 33rd, raised primarily in Hamburg and Bremen. Besides these regions, other troops of the division came from parts of the Province of Hanover adjoining Bremen and Hamburg. Combat chronicle
2 CONT
2 CONT The 17th Reserve Division fought on the Western Front. It fought across Belgium in August 1914 and then occupied the line on the Aisne until September 1915. It then went to Flanders and the Artois, where it remained engaged in pos
2 CONC itional warfare until June 1916. From mid-July to late October 1916, it fought in the Battle of the Somme with only one interlude away from the front.  In 1918, Allied intelligence rated the division as first class.
2 CONT
2 CONT Order of battle on mobilization Memorial of the ‘Infanry Regiment Lübeck (3. hanseatisches) Nr. 162’
2 CONT
2 CONT The order of battle of the 17th Reserve Division on mobilization was as follows:[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT     81. Infanterie-Brigade
2 CONT         Infanterie-Regiment Lübeck (3. Hanseatisches) Nr. 162[4]
2 CONT         Schleswig-Holsteinisches Infanterie-Regiment Nr. 163
2 CONT
2 CONT = 33. Reserve-Infanterie-Brigade=
2 CONT
2 CONT         Reserve-Infanterie-Regiment Nr. 75
2 CONT
2 CONT =Reserve-Infanterie-Regiment Nr. 76=
2 CONT
2 CONT     Reserve-Husaren-Regiment Nr. 6
2 CONT     Reserve-Feldartillerie-Regiment Nr. 17
2 CONT     4.Kompanie/Pionier-Bataillon Nr. 9
2 CONT
2 CONT Order of battle on March 28, 1918
2 CONT
2 CONT The 17th Reserve Division was triangularized in October 1916. Over the course of the war, other changes took place, including the formation of artillery and signals commands and a pioneer battalion. The order of battle on March 28
2 CONC , 1918 was as follows:[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT     81. Reserve-Infanterie-Brigade
2 CONT         Reserve-Infanterie-Regiment Nr. 76
2 CONT         Infanterie-Regiment Lübeck (3. Hanseatisches) Nr. 162
2 CONT         Schleswig-Holsteinisches Infanterie-Regiment Nr. 163
2 CONT     1.Eskadron/Reserve-Husaren-Regiment Nr. 6
2 CONT     Artillerie-Kommandeur 110
2 CONT         Reserve-Feldartillerie-Regiment Nr. 17
2 CONT     Stab Pionier-Bataillon Nr. 317
2 CONT         4.Kompanie/Pionier-Bataillon Nr. 9
2 CONT         Pionier-Kompanie Nr. 340
2 CONT         Minenwerfer-Kompanie Nr. 217
2 CONT     Divisions-Nachrichten-Kommandeur 417
2 CONT
