1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Eintrag im »Gedenkbuch« des Bundesarchivs:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Fränkel, Olga
2 CONT
2 CONT * geb. Marx 
2 CONT * geboren am 01. Oktober 1888 
2 CONT * in Mannheim/Baden
2 CONT
2 CONT * wohnhaft in Berlin 
2 CONT
2 CONT * DEPORTATION
2 CONT * ab Berlin
2 CONT * 14. Dezember 1942, Auschwitz, Konzentrations- und Vernichtungslager
2 CONT
2 CONT cf.: https://www.stolpersteine-berlin.de/en/biografie/9122
2 CONT
2 CONT Olga Fraenkel born Marx, was born to the silk merchant Julius Marx and his wife Ricka. Silbermann, born in Mannheim on October 1, 1888. Her full name was Olga Fanny Fraenkel. She had two brothers, Martin and Adolf. Martin died in 
2 CONC France in 1916 as a soldier in the First World War. At the end of the war in 1918, Adolf returned to Mannheim as a non-commissioned officer. A little later he went to Berlin. There he ran his father's textile trade, which he found
2 CONC ed in Mannheim in 1916, with the Berlin merchant Leo Fraenkel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olga married Leo Fraenkel, the business partner of her brother Adolf Marx. (For him there is a stumbling block at 13 Giesebrechtstrasse). Their daughter Edith Fraenkel was born on February 8, 1922 in Berlin. The marriage soon divo
2 CONC rced and the business partnership ended in 1927.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1934 Olga Fraenkel moved with her daughter Edith to a small apartment on the ground floor at Sächsische Strasse 10/11 in Wilmersdorf. Her written complaints to the "building police" about the lack of ventilation in the windowle
2 CONC ss bathroom got into the building's file and have been preserved in the state archive.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to secure her and her daughter's livelihood, Olga Fraenkel held a paid lunch table for older Jewish women in her apartment. Over the years, the supply situation deteriorated significantly, especially for Jews. Harry Cühn,
2 CONC  who later became the fiance of her daughter Edith, was able to help with the procurement of food. From his memory he described Olga Fraenkel much later as "small, with a dark curly hair, Jewish-looking and quite loud".
2 CONT
2 CONT The lunch table for the 8-10 table ladies every day could be maintained until the first years of the war. For many years, table guests included Doris Tucholsky, mother of Kurt Tucholsky, who died in 1935.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1942, Olga Fraenkel and her daughter had to leave the apartment on Sächsische Strasse. Both were forcibly sent to a so-called "Jewish house" at Pfalzburger Straße 86.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Edith was arrested on July 8, 1942, Olga Fraenkel was allowed to see her daughter one last time in the court prison on Kantstrasse on October 8, 1942. Olga Fraenkel was deported on December 14, 1942 - with the so-called “25t
2 CONC h Osttransport ”to Auschwitz. Her date of death is unknown.
