1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rabbi Shalom Barron
2 CONT (1916 - 9 September 2002)
2 CONT
2 CONT London-born Rev. (later Rabbi) Barron (m. Annie Goldberg of Liverpool) was born into a chassidic family and studied at Liverpool Yeshiva. He served as minister and reader of Nusach Sfard Russell Street Congregation, Liverpool (to 
2 CONC 1939), and for a while was also headmaster at the Southport Hebrew Congregation, Lancashire (1938-1939). He was then appointed as chazan, shochet and teacher of the Darlington Hebrew Congregation (c.1939-1944), and minister to the
2 CONC  Stockport Hebrew Congregation, then in Cheshire (1944-1952). In 1952 Rev. Barron moved to the Irish Republic to become minister and shochet to the Cork Hebrew Congregation (1952-1955). In 1956 he was appointed minister of the Har
2 CONC rismith Hebrew Congregation, South Africa and he served communities at Witbank and Bokburg. Obtaining semicha, Rabbi Barron returned to London in the 1980s and in the 1990s was honorary rabbi to the Machzike Hadass Synagogue in Du
2 CONC blin. Rabbi Shalom Barron retired to Manchester where he died. He was the father of Rabbi Moshe Barron. (Jewish Chronicle press reports and Jewish Year Book listings.)
2 CONT
2 CONT South African Jewry 1967-68 Edition. A Survey of the Jewish Community; Its Contributions to South Africa; Directory of Communal Institutions; and a Who's Who of Leading Personalities Feldberg Leon (Editor) Barron Rabbi Shalom  pag
2 CONC e 181.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jewish Chronicle, 28 June 1940, page 25
2 CONT
2 CONT Engagement. Annie, daughter of the late Mr and Mrs A. S. Goldberg of Liverpool to Rev Shalom Barron, son of the late Mr and Mrs M. Barron, 51 Victoria Road, Darlington. [Marriage. Simon Barron September 1940 Liverpool S 8b 765 //A
2 CONC nnie Goldberg]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jewish Chronicle - July 14 1939
2 CONT
2 CONT DARLINGTON 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rev. Shalom Barron has been elected Chazan-Shochet and Teacher to the Darlington Hebrew Congregation. Mr. Barron was born in London in 1916, and has been Minister and Reader at the Synagogue in Russell Street, Liverpool, and t
2 CONC eacher to the Southport Hebrew Congregation under Rabbi Dr. A. E. Silverstone, Mr. Barron received his secular education at the Liverpool Hebrew Institute, and was awarded many prizes. He also studied at the Liverpool Talmudical C
2 CONC ollege under Rabbis J, J. Unterman, A. J. Krasner, and Z. PHtntck. and attained a high standard in Rabbinics. For a number of years he was President of the Students* Union at the Coliege, and took an active interest in the youth a
2 CONC ctivities in the city,
